# Apple-Inspired UX Rules

## Principles

Apply HIG principles without cloning Apple's visual identity:

- Purpose: every section answers "what does this prove?"
- Agency: users can skip, close, go back, and navigate freely.
- Responsibility: no dark patterns, no forced signup, no hidden data collection.
- Familiarity: standard links, buttons, dialogs, and scroll behavior.
- Flexibility: touch, keyboard, trackpad, screen reader, reduced motion.
- Simplicity: remove anything that does not help the story.
- Craft: alignment, spacing, wording, focus states, and loading states are designed.
- Delight: one memorable moment (the hero), not constant motion.

## Clarity, deference, depth

- Content first. Controls and chrome recede.
- Use materials only for floating functional layers: sticky nav, modal sheet, maybe the 3D frame.
- Do not apply Liquid Glass-style blur to content cards or body text backgrounds.
- Depth must communicate layering or interaction, not decorate empty space.
- Always provide a solid fallback under `prefers-reduced-transparency`.

## Controls and input

- 44x44px minimum hit area; 48px preferred height; 12px spacing between adjacent controls.
- Use native semantics first: `button`, `a`, `dialog`, `details`, `input`.
- Every icon-only control has an accessible name.
- Gestures always have a visible alternative. No swipe-only or drag-only actions.
- Support full keyboard access, visible focus, Escape to close, and focus restoration.
- Do not override browser shortcuts.

## Typography and layout

- Hierarchy through size, weight, spacing, and color - not through many typefaces.
- Body text 16px+ on web; avoid thin weights; keep line length readable.
- Support 200% text zoom and reflow at 320px without clipping or overlap.
- Use safe areas, consistent margins, and predictable content positions.
- Keep primary content near the top/leading edge in reading order.

## Motion and feedback

- Reduced motion: replace movement with fades, disable parallax, pause 3D, stop autoplay.
- Tighten springs; avoid bounce and depth changes on scroll.
- No autoplaying audio/video; no time-boxed UI.
- Feedback must be immediate and explicit: hover, press, loading, success, error, empty.
- Loading states should preserve layout and avoid spinners for sub-300ms waits.
- Errors must say what happened and offer a recovery action.

## Navigation and dialogs

- Sticky nav is compact, translucent only with a solid fallback, and never hides the skip link.
- Mobile menu uses a modal dialog/sheet with focus trap, Escape, scroll lock, and focus return.
- Project carousel is a region, not a mystery meat gesture. Label controls and announce index changes politely.
- Links look like links; buttons look like buttons. Do not style a div as a control.

## Content honesty

- Use real profile/project data only. Never invent metrics, testimonials, clients, awards, or screenshots.
- If an asset or metric is missing, show an honest empty/fallback state.
- Keep external links accurate and mark unavailable projects as case studies or archives.
- Do not use hype copy banned by the SOP.

## Anti-patterns

- Scroll hijacking, forced animation intros, or hidden navigation.
- Blur/glass overload, neon glows, fake depth, or 3D text.
- Tiny controls, hover-only affordances, or color-only status.
- Auto-rotating carousels.
- Modals that trap focus incorrectly or fail to restore it.
- Decorative motion that competes with reading.

## Verification

- Keyboard-only pass on every route.
- Screen-reader smoke test (NVDA or VoiceOver) for nav, carousel, and dialogs.
- Reduced Motion and Reduce Transparency emulation.
- 200% zoom, 320px reflow, and forced-colors mode.
- `axe` scan: no critical or serious violations.

